Constructor & Destructor in Inheritance

📌 Description

This C++ program demonstrates the order of execution of constructors and destructors in inheritance.

The program contains two classes:

- "Base" → Parent/Base class
- "Derived" → Child/Derived class

The "Derived" class inherits from the "Base" class.

---

📊 Class Structure

Base
  ↓
Derived

---

📚 Base Class

The "Base" class contains a constructor and a destructor.

Constructor

Base() {
    std::cout << "Base constructor\n";
}

The constructor is automatically called when a "Base" object or a derived object is created.

Destructor

~Base() {
    std::cout << "Base destructor\n";
}

The destructor is automatically called when the object is destroyed.

---

📚 Derived Class

The "Derived" class inherits from "Base".

It contains its own constructor and destructor.

Constructor

Derived() {
    std::cout << "Derived constructor\n";
}

Destructor

~Derived() {
    std::cout << "Derived destructor\n";
}

---

⚙️ Main Function

A "Derived" object is created:

Derived object;

Object Creation

When the "Derived" object is created:

1. "Base" constructor executes first.
2. "Derived" constructor executes second.

Object Destruction

When the object goes out of scope:

1. "Derived" destructor executes first.
2. "Base" destructor executes second.

---

🖥️ Output

Base constructor
Derived constructor
Derived destructor
Base destructor

---

🧠 Important Concept

Constructor Order

Base → Derived

Base Constructor
      ↓
Derived Constructor

Destructor Order

Derived → Base

Derived Destructor
      ↓
Base Destructor

---

🔑 Key Concepts Demonstrated

- Inheritance
- Base and Derived Classes
- Constructors
- Destructors
- Constructor Execution Order
- Destructor Execution Order
- Object Creation and Destruction
- Code Reusability

«Rule: In inheritance, constructors execute from Base → Derived, while destructors execute in the reverse order, Derived → Base.»
